==============================================================================
DISSECTION of the random 'breaker' S where DIP>1/q-1/14 but M(S)>=1/14
==============================================================================
S = [1, 4, 6, 8, 10, 13, 14, 15, 18, 22, 24, 25, 27]
A = S\{14} = [1, 4, 6, 8, 10, 13, 15, 18, 22, 24, 25, 27]
M(S) = 4/37 (0.108108) at tau*=7/37;  >=1/14 ? True
M(A) = 2/7 (0.285714) at tau*=3/7
smallest uncovered q of A = 7;  1/q = 1/7 (0.142857)
  Is M(A) > 1/q (core gap beats trivial witness)?  True  [M(A)=2/7 vs 1/q=1/7]
  stated slack 1/q-1/14 = 1/14 (0.071429)
  TRUE slack M(A)-1/14  = 3/14 (0.214286)
  DIP = M(A)-M(S) = 46/259 (0.177606)
  => DIP <= 1/q-1/14 (stated)? False
  => DIP <= M(A)-1/14 (true)?  True  (this is just M(S)>=1/14)
CONCLUSION: the stated certificate fails ONLY because 1/q UNDERSTATES M(A).
The core is EASIER than the trivial witness shows; true slack is bigger.

